+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See `proposal.md` for motivation. Current state this design works with:
+
+- `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` §1 is the only place in that document claiming platform
+  verification: it says the `v0.1.0` release "provides verified Windows and Ubuntu x86-64
+  archives" and routes "macOS or another platform" to a source build. There is no separate
+  verified-platforms table in the file.
+- The `v0.1.0` release ships exactly two archives — `windows-x86_64.zip` and
+  `linux-x86_64.tar.gz` (`docs/releases/0.1.0.md`). No `windows-aarch64` archive exists, so on an
+  ARM64 laptop the only install path is the documented source build:
+  `cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli`, then `graphhelm --version`.
+- This repository runs no hosted CI ("GitHub Actions are intentionally disabled" per the release
+  notes), so platform verification is a local, recorded run: the acceptance convention is a dated
+  file under `docs/acceptance/` with a host/commit/result header table and a command transcript
+  (format of `docs/acceptance/install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`, cited by GETTING_STARTED.md).
+- `graphhelm --version` is clap-derived from the workspace version in `Cargo.toml`
+  (`version = "0.1.0"`), so at the current tree it prints `graphhelm 0.1.0`. The `VERSION` file
+  says `0.1.1-spec`; the observable value is the Cargo workspace one.
+- The machine used to plan and check in this change is **not** the observer: Windows 11 Pro on an
+  AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (AMD64). The promise ("installed and `graphhelm --version` runs on a
+  Snapdragon laptop") can only be observed on the laptop itself.
+- Keel rule 5 / the Journey-Proven rules of `AGENTS.md`: a proxy (another OS, an emulator, a VM,
+  a cross-compile) is not an observation; an unobserved promise ends `OBSERVER_MISSING`.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- A one-time, reproducible rehearsal recipe that produces the required evidence on a Windows 11
+  ARM64 (aarch64, Snapdragon) laptop: source install of the CLI and a successful `graphhelm
+  --version` from the installed binary.
+- A checked-in acceptance record for that run, following the existing `docs/acceptance/` format.
+- A documentation edit confined to the verified-platforms statement in `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`,
+  precise about what was exercised.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Shipping a `windows-aarch64` release archive or touching `docs/releases/0.1.0.md`'s archive
+  table (that is a release change, not a documentation claim).
+- Rehearsing the full getting-started journey (init, serve, Studio, harness) on ARM64; the claim
+  covers exactly what is exercised.
+- Adding an ARM64 job to any CI matrix (no hosted CI exists).
+- Performance benchmarking or comparison with x86-64 build/run times.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **D1 — Observer: native bare-metal Windows 11 ARM64 laptop (Snapdragon).**
+  Why: the promise names that hardware class, and every substitute is a proxy under the
+  repository's proof rules. Alternatives considered and rejected: cross-compiling
+  `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` from the AMD64 planning host (proves compilation, not execution;
+  linking also needs the MSVC ARM64 cross toolchain); WSL2/emulated runs (proxies); a hosted arm64
+  runner (none exists — hosted CI is intentionally off).
+- **D2 — Install path: the documented source build.** `cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path
+  apps/cli` exactly as GETTING_STARTED.md §1 gives it, because no ARM64 archive ships in `v0.1.0`.
+  Alternative considered: cut a new ARM64 archive now — rejected, out of scope (release artifact,
+  separate change).
+- **D3 — Evidence record: new `docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`.** Format copied
+  from `install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`: header table (commit under test, host identity from
+  `systeminfo` / `(Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor).Name` / `$env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` / OS
+  build, source, started/ended, result) plus the command transcript with outputs, elisions marked.
+  The date in the filename is the rehearsal date, matching the naming convention already used in
+  `docs/acceptance/`.
+- **D4 — Doc edit: amend the §1 statement in place; do not invent a new section layout.** The
+  sentence at lines ~88–92 becomes: the release archives verify Windows x86-64 and Ubuntu x86-64;
+  Windows 11 ARM64 (aarch64, e.g., Snapdragon laptops) is verified **from source** with a link to
+  the acceptance record; other platforms still build from source. Precise wording about scope
+  (install + version) follows the spec's claim requirement. Layout (sentence vs. small table) is a
+  styling choice open to reviewer preference; the behavior contract is the spec's, not the layout.
+- **D5 — Observed commands (the evidence set).** Prerequisite checks as listed in GETTING_STARTED.md
+  (`cargo +1.97.1 --version`, `git --version`, PATH state); clone/copy of the exact commit under
+  test; the `cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli` line; `(Get-Command graphhelm).Source`
+  to show the binary is the installed one on PATH; `graphhelm --version` expecting
+  `graphhelm 0.1.0`. To match the bar the `v0.1.0` "Release checks" apply to its binaries, also
+  capture `graphhelm --help` and one canonical offline command
+  (`graph validate examples/graphs/software-feature.yaml`) — but the mandatory promise remains
+  install + `--version`; the extras are evidence polish the transcript makes cheap.
+- **D6 — No code or configuration changes reach a build.** A Markdown-only diff needs no cargo
+  check on the planning host (Keel: "a Markdown edit that no code reads needs no build"). The
+  smallest checks observing the change: re-read the edited text against the record; `git diff
+  --check` and a scoped `git diff --stat` confirming only docs touched. The compile+run happens on
+  the laptop, and its transcript is itself the observation — not something re-run here.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [No ARM64 laptop available when apply starts] → the change ends `OBSERVER_MISSING: Windows 11
+  ARM64 (aarch64, Snapdragon) laptop` and no doc entry lands; the rehearsal recipe stays reusable.
+  Mitigation: tasks order hardware availability first, so nothing else is spent before it.
+- [First ARM64 native build fails (toolchain/linker gap specific to aarch64-pc-windows-msvc)] → an
+  honest `OBSERVER_MISSING` plus a defect named in the acceptance record (or issue) is the correct
+  outcome; the doc claim is not weakened to absorb the failure.
+- [Prerequisite gap on the fresh ARM64 host, e.g. MSVC C++ Build Tools needed for the linker while
+  GETTING_STARTED.md's Windows block does not list them] → fix the Windows prerequisites text in
+  the same change only if the rehearsal actually hit it; otherwise record it as observed state.
+- [Version drift between `VERSION` (`0.1.1-spec`) and the Cargo workspace version (`0.1.0`)] → the
+  record asserts the expected line against the commit under test, so it stays true; the doc entry
+  says "`graphhelm --version` prints the workspace version" rather than pinning prose to a number
+  that will move.
+- [The claim reads narrower than the x86-64 claim (archive install + release-check battery vs.
+  source install + version)] → accepted: precision over symmetry is the point of this capability;
+  widening the exercise set is a future change.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Not applicable beyond rollback: revert the documentation commit and delete the acceptance record to
+restore the pre-change claims. No data, state, or compatibility surface is involved.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- The exact model/SoC of the owner's Snapdragon laptop and whether it is reachable at apply time.
+  Deferrable: the acceptance record captures whatever identity the host reports; the task breakdown
+  does not change either way (unreachable → `OBSERVER_MISSING`).
+- Whether maintainers prefer a dedicated "Verified platforms" subsection/table in GETTING_STARTED.md
+  over an in-place sentence amendment. Deferrable: D4 fixes the behavior contract; layout is a
+  review-time style choice.
