+# Spec Delta
+
+## Purpose
+
+Defines which platforms are claimed as verified for installing and running the `graphhelm` CLI, and the evidence bar every such claim must carry in the install documentation.
+
+## ADDED Requirements
+
+### Requirement: Verified platforms are listed in the install documentation
+
+`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` SHALL name every platform claimed as verified for installing and running the `graphhelm` CLI, and for each one SHALL state the verified install path (release archive or source build) and link the checked-in acceptance record under `docs/acceptance/` that carries the evidence.
+
+#### Scenario: Windows ARM64 appears as verified
+
+- **WHEN** this change is applied and section 1 of `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` is read
+- **THEN** Windows ARM64 (aarch64, e.g., Snapdragon laptops) is named as verified
+- **AND** the entry states the source build as the verified install path
+- **AND** it links an acceptance record that exists in the repository
+
+#### Scenario: Existing archive claim is preserved
+
+- **WHEN** the document names the `v0.1.0` release archives as verified
+- **THEN** it still lists only the platforms those archives cover (Windows x86-64, Ubuntu x86-64), consistent with `docs/releases/0.1.0.md`
+
+### Requirement: Every verified-platform claim carries an evidence record
+
+A platform MAY be added to the verified list only together with a dated acceptance record committed under `docs/acceptance/` in the same change. The record SHALL identify the host hardware (OS edition and version, architecture, SoC or CPU model), the commit under test, and contain the installation commands as executed plus the output of `graphhelm --version`.
+
+#### Scenario: Record fields present
+
+- **WHEN** the Windows ARM64 acceptance record is reviewed
+- **THEN** it names the OS edition and version, the aarch64 architecture, the SoC or CPU model, and the commit under test
+- **AND** its transcript includes the `cargo` install command and a `graphhelm --version` line
+
+#### Scenario: Platform listed without its record
+
+- **WHEN** a documentation change adds a platform to the verified list
+- **AND** no acceptance record it links exists in the repository at review time
+- **THEN** the addition is rejected and the platform stays unlisted
+
+### Requirement: Verification runs on native bare-metal hardware
+
+A platform's verification SHALL be observed on native bare-metal hardware of the claimed operating system and architecture. Cross-compiling from another host, OS-level emulation, virtual machines, and containers are not observers for a platform claim; they may be run, but their results SHALL be reported as unobserved for the claim, never as verification.
+
+#### Scenario: Cross-compiled binary does not verify the platform
+
+- **WHEN** a binary is built for the `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` target on an x86-64 Windows machine and is never executed on ARM64 hardware
+- **THEN** Windows ARM64 is not added to the verified list and the outcome is recorded as not-verified
+
+#### Scenario: Emulated execution does not verify the platform
+
+- **WHEN** `graphhelm --version` succeeds only under emulation, a virtual machine, or WSL
+- **THEN** the run is reported as unobserved for the native-hardware claim
+
+### Requirement: The claim states exactly what was exercised
+
+Each verified-platform entry SHALL name the operations it covers, and the documentation MUST NOT imply operations beyond those exercised. For this change the Windows ARM64 entry covers installing the CLI from source and `graphhelm --version` printing `graphhelm <workspace version>` — the workspace version of the commit under test (`0.1.0` at planning time).
+
+#### Scenario: Version output matches the workspace version
+
+- **WHEN** `graphhelm --version` is run on the verified ARM64 host
+- **THEN** it prints `graphhelm 0.1.0` for a commit whose workspace version is `0.1.0`
+
+#### Scenario: Unexercised parts are not implied
+
+- **WHEN** the ARM64 entry states the source install and the version command only
+- **THEN** the entry does not claim parts of the getting-started journey that were not run on that host
+
+### Requirement: Missing observer is a recorded outcome
+
+When the required hardware for a platform claim is not available, the work SHALL end by reporting `OBSERVER_MISSING` naming the missing host, and the documentation SHALL NOT gain a verified-platform entry for that platform.
+
+#### Scenario: Snapdragon laptop unavailable
+
+- **WHEN** the rehearsal cannot be run on a Windows 11 ARM64 (aarch64, Snapdragon) laptop before the change is completed
+- **THEN** the completion report ends `OBSERVER_MISSING: Windows 11 ARM64 (aarch64, Snapdragon) laptop`
+- **AND** `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` carries no Windows ARM64 entry
