+# Tasks
+
+## 1. Observer host and prerequisites (on the Windows 11 ARM64 laptop)
+
+- [ ] 1.1 Confirm a Windows 11 ARM64 (aarch64, Snapdragon) laptop is available and power it through
+      host identification: capture OS edition/build (`systeminfo`), CPU/SoC model
+      (`(Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor).Name`), and `$env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE`. Verify:
+      `$env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` reads `ARM64` and the SoC is recorded; if no such laptop is
+      reachable, STOP here — report `OBSERVER_MISSING: Windows 11 ARM64 (aarch64, Snapdragon)
+      laptop`, leave `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` untouched, and do not proceed to any later
+      group.
+- [ ] 1.2 Install the documented prerequisites on that host exactly as
+      `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` §Prerequisites gives them (Rustup via winget,
+      `rustup toolchain install 1.97.1 --profile minimal --component rustfmt clippy`, git).
+      Verify: `cargo +1.97.1 --version` prints the pinned version, `git --version` answers, and a
+      fresh shell finds `graphhelm` nowhere yet (`Get-Command graphhelm` fails). Note every
+      prerequisite the guide did not list but the host actually needed (for example MSVC C++ Build
+      Tools for the linker) with the exact error — that note drives task 3.2.
+
+## 2. Rehearsal: install the CLI and observe `graphhelm --version` (on the laptop)
+
+- [ ] 2.1 Name the commit under test: record the exact commit (full sha of the current `main` tip)
+      whose tree will be built. Verify: the sha is written down before any build starts and will be
+      the "Commit under test" of the acceptance record.
+- [ ] 2.2 Obtain that exact tree on the laptop (clone or copy of 2.1's commit — not a later one)
+      and run the documented install: `cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli`. Verify:
+      exit code 0, `graphhelm.exe` present in `%USERPROFILE%\.cargo\bin`, transcript line kept.
+- [ ] 2.3 Prove the observed binary is the installed one and runs: `(Get-Command graphhelm).Source`
+      then `graphhelm --version`. Verify: the Source path is
+      `%USERPROFILE%\.cargo\bin\graphhelm.exe` and the last line is exactly `graphhelm 0.1.0`
+      (the workspace version of the commit under test — if the recorded sha carries a different
+      workspace version, the expectation is `graphhelm <that version>` and both values are noted).
+- [ ] 2.4 Capture the release-check parity extras from the clone root with the installed binary:
+      `graphhelm --help`, then `graphhelm graph validate examples/graphs/software-feature.yaml`.
+      Verify: `--help` lists the command groups, and the validate run returns the
+      `"ok": true` envelope for the example graph; both transcripts kept (these are evidence
+      polish beyond the install + version promise, per design D5).
+
+## 3. Evidence record and documentation (in this checkout)
+
+- [ ] 3.1 Write `docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md` (rehearsal date, format copied
+      from `docs/acceptance/install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`): header table with commit under test
+      (2.1), host identity (1.1), source, started/ended times, result **pass**, secrets policy
+      (none expected — token/key never enter this recipe); transcript sections covering tasks
+      1.2–2.4 as executed, elisions marked. Verify: the file satisfies the spec's "Record fields
+      present" scenario — OS edition/version, aarch64 architecture, SoC/CPU model, commit, the
+      `cargo` install command, and the `graphhelm --version` line are all present and true.
+- [ ] 3.2 Amend section 1 of `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`: add Windows ARM64 (aarch64, e.g.,
+      Snapdragon laptops) to the verified-platform statement as verified **from source** with a
+      relative link to the new acceptance record; keep the existing verified-archive claim limited
+      to Windows x86-64 / Ubuntu x86-64 and keep the build-from-source routing for other
+      platforms; scope the entry to exactly the operations exercised (source install +
+      `graphhelm --version`, per the spec). If task 1.2 recorded an undocumented prerequisite the
+      rehearsal actually needed, add it to the Windows prerequisites text in the same edit — and
+      only then. Verify: re-read the edited section end-to-end; the claim names what was
+      exercised only; `git diff --stat` shows only `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` plus the
+      acceptance record touched so far.
+- [ ] 3.3 Cross-check the pair before finishing: the GETTING_STARTED.md link resolves to the
+      acceptance file that exists in the tree, the record's stated host matches what the record's
+      own transcript reported, and the record's commit under test equals the tree that was built.
+      Verify: `git status` shows the intended two documentation files; opening the link target and
+      comparing host/commit lines passes by eye; `git diff --check` is clean.
+
+## 4. Completion report
+
+- [ ] 4.1 State the outcome separately, per the repository's proof rules: passed = the rehearsal
+      transcript observations (2.2–2.4) with commands named; failed/skipped = any task that was
+      not reached; unobserved = the rest of the getting-started journey on ARM64 (init, serve,
+      Studio, harness) which this change deliberately does not claim. Verify: the report names
+      every executed command and its result, and asserts no code or configuration file changed
+      (`git diff --name-only` lists documentation only, so no build or test suite was run on the
+      planning host — a Markdown-only diff reaches none).
+
+## Workflow follow-up
+
+- Archive the change after the project's review requirements are satisfied:
+  `openspec archive bench-feature`, which promotes the delta to the main spec at
+  `openspec/specs/install/verified-platforms/spec.md`.
+- If task 1.1 stopped with `OBSERVER_MISSING`, leave the change open (do not archive); the
+  recipe in groups 1–3 stands ready for the next time the laptop is available.
